WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.750 --> 00:00:11.230
RON PANTOFARO: So let's go
10,000 feet up in the air

00:00:11.230 --> 00:00:13.030
and take a look at migrations.

00:00:13.030 --> 00:00:13.530
Right.

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:15.980
So migration is a journey.

00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:18.280
And the journey
always starts with

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:21.250
a careful and thorough
assessment of the environments

00:00:21.250 --> 00:00:22.400
you want to move.

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:23.140
All right.

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:27.310
And then you do what we
call cloud foundations.

00:00:27.310 --> 00:00:32.530
Cloud foundations we mean you
start networking preparations,

00:00:32.530 --> 00:00:35.200
you do identity
management, project

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:38.780
an organizational
structure, and so on.

00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:41.290
And then there is this little
task of actually moving

00:00:41.290 --> 00:00:43.900
your workloads into GCP.

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:44.890
And then you optimize.

00:00:44.890 --> 00:00:48.610
When we say optimize, we
mean you can reduce costs.

00:00:48.610 --> 00:00:50.080
You can improve performance.

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:51.970
You can leverage
managed services

00:00:51.970 --> 00:00:57.377
or use cloud native
technologies, whatever works.

00:00:57.377 --> 00:00:59.210
TAD EINSTEIN: So we
talked about assessment.

00:00:59.210 --> 00:01:00.885
Ron mentioned that's
the first one.

00:01:00.885 --> 00:01:02.510
But actually what we
want to enable you

00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:05.209
with is prior to the assessment
before you start deploying

00:01:05.209 --> 00:01:06.890
an assessment tool
and understand what's

00:01:06.890 --> 00:01:08.598
in the environment,
it's really important

00:01:08.598 --> 00:01:10.760
to understand what
your cloud strategy is.

00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:13.100
So Google's professional
services organization

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:15.950
has released something known as
the GCAF, which is the Google

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:17.720
Cloud adoption framework.

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:19.380
I recommend everyone
download this.

00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:20.233
It's a white paper.

00:01:20.233 --> 00:01:21.650
And what it does
is it really goes

00:01:21.650 --> 00:01:25.010
over all the major key items
that you need to focus on

00:01:25.010 --> 00:01:27.170
as you're looking to
take your cloud journey,

00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:29.660
everything from culture
change to making sure

00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:33.200
that everyone has the right
skill sets, technologies,

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:34.940
and really helping
the business define.

00:01:34.940 --> 00:01:37.070
And I usually say, when
I work with my customers,

00:01:37.070 --> 00:01:38.570
I bring this usually
to the C-suite.

00:01:38.570 --> 00:01:40.940
We're able to have some
very deep discussions.

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:42.863
And, really, at any level
of the organization,

00:01:42.863 --> 00:01:45.530
it really helps you plan so that
when you go into the assessment

00:01:45.530 --> 00:01:47.613
phase, you're going to get
what you're looking for

00:01:47.613 --> 00:01:50.910
and you the right strategy
from a corporate standpoint.

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:52.910
RON PANTOFARO: And speaking
of assessment phase,

00:01:52.910 --> 00:01:55.850
let's talk a little bit what's
happening during assessment

00:01:55.850 --> 00:01:56.960
and discovery.

00:01:56.960 --> 00:01:59.850
So there's enablement
and training.

00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:00.350
Right.

00:02:00.350 --> 00:02:01.950
JCP is a new platform.

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:05.150
You might need new tool
sets, new skill sets.

00:02:05.150 --> 00:02:07.520
You might want to do
some proof of concept.

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:11.090
You take inventory of
applications, workloads,

00:02:11.090 --> 00:02:17.120
servers, licenses, operating
systems, the works.

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:19.610
You start thinking
about costs, especially

00:02:19.610 --> 00:02:21.200
if you move from on prem.

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:23.000
It's a different
financial model.

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:26.060
You do some dependency mappings
between the applications

00:02:26.060 --> 00:02:27.510
and the workloads.

00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:30.560
And then you start
thinking about how

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:33.110
you catalog the
applications and which

00:02:33.110 --> 00:02:37.310
ones you're going to
move first to the cloud.

00:02:37.310 --> 00:02:42.230
And this is an example of one
way to catalog applications.

00:02:42.230 --> 00:02:47.450
So we got applications that
are what we call disconnected,

00:02:47.450 --> 00:02:49.700
meaning they don't
have any dependencies.

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:51.710
And they're not
mission critical.

00:02:51.710 --> 00:02:54.020
And those are relatively easy.

00:02:54.020 --> 00:02:56.750
Then you've got to ones
that have some dependencies

00:02:56.750 --> 00:03:01.310
but are still not mission
critical like a back office.

00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:02.870
Gets trickier.

00:03:02.870 --> 00:03:05.960
And then you got production
workloads, your ERP,

00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:09.410
and your databases, and
your production apps.

00:03:09.410 --> 00:03:13.310
And they obviously
have cross dependencies

00:03:13.310 --> 00:03:14.900
and are mission critical.

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:19.723
And those get hard or
sometimes impossible to move.

00:03:19.723 --> 00:03:22.140
TAD EINSTEIN: So when you go
through the assessment phase,

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:24.650
you're going to get to
learn about the two tools

00:03:24.650 --> 00:03:26.150
that we've partnered with.

00:03:26.150 --> 00:03:29.180
We talk about characteristics
of a good first mover.

00:03:29.180 --> 00:03:30.680
And what I'm going
to do here is I'm

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:32.680
going to highlight just
a few that are critical.

00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:34.663
So for example, a lot of
companies go into this

00:03:34.663 --> 00:03:36.080
and they say, you
know what, if we

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:38.133
can move our largest
application, that's

00:03:38.133 --> 00:03:39.050
going to be a success.

00:03:39.050 --> 00:03:40.580
And companies do this.

00:03:40.580 --> 00:03:41.463
I don't recommend it.

00:03:41.463 --> 00:03:43.130
I usually recommend
you choose something

00:03:43.130 --> 00:03:44.387
that's not business critical.

00:03:44.387 --> 00:03:46.470
There's always a lot of
risk when you get started.

00:03:46.470 --> 00:03:48.650
There's a lot of learning
usually on your side.

00:03:48.650 --> 00:03:50.900
And you want to make sure
you do something that really

00:03:50.900 --> 00:03:53.000
can be consumed and
that you reduce risk,

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:53.570
because at the
end of the day you

00:03:53.570 --> 00:03:56.153
want to start proving out that
Cloud's the right move for you,

00:03:56.153 --> 00:03:58.520
that you really understand
how to properly migrate.

00:03:58.520 --> 00:04:00.560
And that's a good
direction to go in.

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:03.670
Also to highlight you don't
want to choose edge cases.

00:04:03.670 --> 00:04:04.170
Right.

00:04:04.170 --> 00:04:06.545
A lot of customers have that
really difficult application

00:04:06.545 --> 00:04:08.000
and they'd like to use that.

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:09.770
It's not good and here's why.

00:04:09.770 --> 00:04:11.150
When you do the
first migrations,

00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:12.860
you want do something
that's repeatable,

00:04:12.860 --> 00:04:14.190
because everyone
the organization

00:04:14.190 --> 00:04:15.740
is going to be doing
this for the first time.

00:04:15.740 --> 00:04:17.115
So you want an
application that's

00:04:17.115 --> 00:04:18.860
very representative
of what may be

00:04:18.860 --> 00:04:22.553
a majority or a good percentage
of your applications are.

00:04:22.553 --> 00:04:24.470
Some other ones to really
focus on I would say

00:04:24.470 --> 00:04:27.260
are requiring less app
refactoring and requiring

00:04:27.260 --> 00:04:29.870
less data migration, because
you want to get a quick win.

00:04:29.870 --> 00:04:31.940
You want to get
comfortable with the cloud.

00:04:31.940 --> 00:04:34.172
Through one of our
tools Velostrata,

00:04:34.172 --> 00:04:35.880
when you talk about
having a lot of data,

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:38.213
you don't have to worry about
it because the VM comes up

00:04:38.213 --> 00:04:39.885
in Google Cloud as
it streams the data.

00:04:39.885 --> 00:04:41.510
But still, nonetheless
choose something

00:04:41.510 --> 00:04:43.427
with a small enough data
set so you can really

00:04:43.427 --> 00:04:45.580
get that win quicker than not.

00:04:45.580 --> 00:04:47.330
And the last one
just to highlight

00:04:47.330 --> 00:04:49.170
is compliance challenges.

00:04:49.170 --> 00:04:51.080
So I personally have
been in situations

00:04:51.080 --> 00:04:53.360
where a customer says
we have the perfect app.

00:04:53.360 --> 00:04:54.770
Everyone goes forward.

00:04:54.770 --> 00:04:56.530
And then compliance
comes and says

00:04:56.530 --> 00:04:59.000
this one's highly regulated
and it takes about eight months

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:00.182
to work through compliance.

00:05:00.182 --> 00:05:01.640
So these are the
kinds of questions

00:05:01.640 --> 00:05:03.710
you want to ask internally
as you're working

00:05:03.710 --> 00:05:07.170
and also as you work
with a Google partner.

00:05:07.170 --> 00:05:08.760
So how do we make this happen?

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:10.640
Well, we have four
migration partners--

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:13.610
Cloud Physics, StratoZone,
both of who are here today,

00:05:13.610 --> 00:05:15.590
and also RISC Networks
and Cloudamize.

00:05:15.590 --> 00:05:17.090
And this is really
going to give you

00:05:17.090 --> 00:05:18.920
the tools to understand
deeply what's

00:05:18.920 --> 00:05:21.350
in your environment
from a cost perspective

00:05:21.350 --> 00:05:23.060
and also from a
component perspective

00:05:23.060 --> 00:05:25.940
as well as connectivity
between those components.

00:05:25.940 --> 00:05:27.530
So with that being
said, I'd like

00:05:27.530 --> 00:05:30.643
to introduce Chris Grossmeier
from Cloud Physics.

00:05:30.643 --> 00:05:32.060
CHRIS GROSSMEIER:
So Cloud Physics

00:05:32.060 --> 00:05:33.590
is really the
first step when you

00:05:33.590 --> 00:05:35.600
get to that level of
assessment that you

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:37.475
want to make that decision
to go to the cloud

00:05:37.475 --> 00:05:38.600
or not go to the cloud.

00:05:38.600 --> 00:05:39.500
At some point
you're going to have

00:05:39.500 --> 00:05:41.450
to have a marker or
an indicator telling

00:05:41.450 --> 00:05:43.070
you is this an opportunity.

00:05:43.070 --> 00:05:45.870
So when we look at the
ecosystems of users out there,

00:05:45.870 --> 00:05:47.900
the platform's a
little bit more broad.

00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:49.550
IT users use this day to day.

00:05:49.550 --> 00:05:52.490
Our pedigree started as a
vSphere administrative tool.

00:05:52.490 --> 00:05:54.020
Great insights into
what's going on

00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:56.103
in the virtual infrastructure,
a lot of analytics,

00:05:56.103 --> 00:05:57.935
and right sizing,
and functionality,

00:05:57.935 --> 00:06:00.560
really giving you meaningful use
as a vSphere admin in the data

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:01.610
center.

00:06:01.610 --> 00:06:03.170
If you're a channel
partner, this

00:06:03.170 --> 00:06:05.940
is really the next level up that
we're seeing a lot of activity.

00:06:05.940 --> 00:06:07.700
This is that trusted
advisor, the team

00:06:07.700 --> 00:06:09.492
or the partner that's
coming in and helping

00:06:09.492 --> 00:06:11.868
you make that intelligent
decision to go to the cloud.

00:06:11.868 --> 00:06:12.410
They come in.

00:06:12.410 --> 00:06:15.800
They may offer the assessment
for you, be fully funded.

00:06:15.800 --> 00:06:18.600
They may offer hardware
refresh, right sizing,

00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:20.400
and other services to
help you get there.

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:22.500
They're really critical to
making sure that that happens.

00:06:22.500 --> 00:06:23.570
And again, like I
said, that trusted

00:06:23.570 --> 00:06:25.570
advisor and that collaborative
intelligence view

00:06:25.570 --> 00:06:26.680
between the two of you.

00:06:26.680 --> 00:06:30.350
And ultimately, this third
tier also, Google, Intel,

00:06:30.350 --> 00:06:31.820
funding these types
of assessments

00:06:31.820 --> 00:06:34.580
and helping you guys
make the right move.

00:06:34.580 --> 00:06:37.250
Reference architectures,
best practices,

00:06:37.250 --> 00:06:39.590
showing services that have
been repeatable and usable

00:06:39.590 --> 00:06:40.577
in the past.

00:06:40.577 --> 00:06:42.410
Combined, these are all
the channel partners

00:06:42.410 --> 00:06:43.880
and the resources
that are bringing

00:06:43.880 --> 00:06:45.380
these types of
assessments to market

00:06:45.380 --> 00:06:48.632
so you don't have to feel
like you're doing this alone.

00:06:48.632 --> 00:06:50.030
So we have multiple
use cases I'm

00:06:50.030 --> 00:06:52.780
going to talk through
real quick here.

00:06:52.780 --> 00:06:56.103
Getting a view of what's
going on inside my data center

00:06:56.103 --> 00:06:57.520
is the first part
of the platform.

00:06:57.520 --> 00:06:59.290
But what's nice is, once
we have performance data

00:06:59.290 --> 00:07:01.030
and configuration
data, we can start

00:07:01.030 --> 00:07:02.522
doing costing and modeling.

00:07:02.522 --> 00:07:04.480
One of the core
functionalities of the platform

00:07:04.480 --> 00:07:07.330
allows us to model
approximate costs of resources

00:07:07.330 --> 00:07:09.340
and estimates of how
much does our data center

00:07:09.340 --> 00:07:11.250
cost to operate today.

00:07:11.250 --> 00:07:14.110
Intelligent meaning of that
value is that we can look at it

00:07:14.110 --> 00:07:16.450
and say, is it going to
save us money or cost us

00:07:16.450 --> 00:07:19.590
more, so this ROI and
TCO type analysis.

00:07:19.590 --> 00:07:22.785
Getting visibility into what's
unique about on premise,

00:07:22.785 --> 00:07:24.160
the hardware, the
resources we're

00:07:24.160 --> 00:07:27.340
consuming, services, and
power, and things like that.

00:07:27.340 --> 00:07:29.440
On the cloud side though,
changing up the regions,

00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:32.590
changing storage options,
changing sizing options

00:07:32.590 --> 00:07:34.727
and right sizing, choosing
different geographies.

00:07:34.727 --> 00:07:36.310
We can apply all
those different costs

00:07:36.310 --> 00:07:39.490
and give you approximations
between the two resources

00:07:39.490 --> 00:07:41.190
as well as other
clouds if that were so

00:07:41.190 --> 00:07:42.820
being in the assessment.

00:07:45.335 --> 00:07:46.335
Ignore the bullets here.

00:07:46.335 --> 00:07:47.293
I'll talk to the image.

00:07:47.293 --> 00:07:49.800
Looks like they get
out of sync here.

00:07:49.800 --> 00:07:52.183
For those of you that
have on premise hardware,

00:07:52.183 --> 00:07:54.100
visibility into the
hardware and the resources

00:07:54.100 --> 00:07:55.440
is also going to be critical.

00:07:55.440 --> 00:07:57.240
As you start aging
out your old systems,

00:07:57.240 --> 00:08:00.870
you're running vSphere
6.0 or maybe 5.5,

00:08:00.870 --> 00:08:03.360
you want to make that
migration to vSphere 6.7.

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:05.610
There's going to be an
opportunity to do some hardware

00:08:05.610 --> 00:08:06.570
refresh potentially.

00:08:06.570 --> 00:08:08.052
And what you really
want to start

00:08:08.052 --> 00:08:10.260
is looking at the story
that's relevant to this cloud

00:08:10.260 --> 00:08:10.920
migration.

00:08:10.920 --> 00:08:13.980
Should I replace this hardware
and bring it up to 6.7

00:08:13.980 --> 00:08:17.160
and invest those resources,
or is there a cost avoidance

00:08:17.160 --> 00:08:18.405
by moving to the cloud?

00:08:18.405 --> 00:08:20.280
And having the intelligence
behind the scenes

00:08:20.280 --> 00:08:22.655
as to the number of resources
and what potentially should

00:08:22.655 --> 00:08:25.464
move is going to be important.

00:08:25.464 --> 00:08:27.420
And the one that's
out of order here

00:08:27.420 --> 00:08:29.970
that show migration
to AWS scenario.

00:08:29.970 --> 00:08:33.600
We can actually pull in the live
feed from that EC2, CloudWatch,

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:35.580
your billing
resources, and start

00:08:35.580 --> 00:08:38.280
looking at the cost
and the spend on AWS

00:08:38.280 --> 00:08:43.500
and give you a comparison
of resources on GCP.

00:08:43.500 --> 00:08:45.630
Looking at the instances,
identifying which

00:08:45.630 --> 00:08:46.848
would be the ideal class.

00:08:46.848 --> 00:08:48.390
Maybe we want to do
some right sizing

00:08:48.390 --> 00:08:51.095
and adjust based on performance,
taking some of the waste

00:08:51.095 --> 00:08:52.470
that we have on
the other clouds.

00:08:52.470 --> 00:08:55.290
As we've seen typically
of resources deployed,

00:08:55.290 --> 00:08:58.710
once it's out there, it's very
rarely adjusted or right sized,

00:08:58.710 --> 00:09:00.180
taking those types
of intelligence

00:09:00.180 --> 00:09:01.972
and making that compelling
story internally

00:09:01.972 --> 00:09:04.540
for that cloud migration.

00:09:04.540 --> 00:09:07.240
And finally some financial
and performance analysis.

00:09:07.240 --> 00:09:09.070
There's a lot more
inside the data

00:09:09.070 --> 00:09:12.070
center that may have an impact
on their decision to move--

00:09:12.070 --> 00:09:14.380
storage, for example,
storage waste, resources,

00:09:14.380 --> 00:09:18.128
and hardware refresh; licenses
and third party resources

00:09:18.128 --> 00:09:18.920
in the data center.

00:09:18.920 --> 00:09:20.462
How efficiently is
your team actually

00:09:20.462 --> 00:09:22.280
managing the data as well.

00:09:22.280 --> 00:09:23.950
Like I said, our
pedigree started off

00:09:23.950 --> 00:09:26.090
as a vSphere admin tool.

00:09:26.090 --> 00:09:27.970
A lot of the best
practices and resources,

00:09:27.970 --> 00:09:29.952
patching and management.

00:09:29.952 --> 00:09:31.660
Gaining that visibility
and understanding

00:09:31.660 --> 00:09:33.850
how efficiently you actually
manage your data center

00:09:33.850 --> 00:09:35.030
may be important.

00:09:35.030 --> 00:09:37.020
You may find that you
are doing a excellent job

00:09:37.020 --> 00:09:39.520
and you can actually keep things
on premise very efficiently

00:09:39.520 --> 00:09:40.937
while you do the
migration, or you

00:09:40.937 --> 00:09:43.068
may find that you are
way behind the times.

00:09:43.068 --> 00:09:44.110
You're not doing support.

00:09:44.110 --> 00:09:45.020
You're not doing updates.

00:09:45.020 --> 00:09:46.870
There's a lot of security
issues out there.

00:09:46.870 --> 00:09:48.490
Maybe it's time to
move to the cloud

00:09:48.490 --> 00:09:53.400
and let the cloud providers
take on that risk for you.

00:09:53.400 --> 00:09:56.310
So architecturally, quickly
address how this works.

00:09:56.310 --> 00:09:57.680
Cloud Physics is an appliance.

00:09:57.680 --> 00:10:01.110
We're a single virtual appliance
purview center or a connection

00:10:01.110 --> 00:10:02.193
into the cloud provider.

00:10:02.193 --> 00:10:04.110
And what we'll do is
we'll collect performance

00:10:04.110 --> 00:10:07.055
and configuration metadata
from the resources

00:10:07.055 --> 00:10:08.680
and bring it to the
cloud for analysis.

00:10:08.680 --> 00:10:10.773
So we are a SaaS based platform.

00:10:10.773 --> 00:10:13.440
We will continue to collect that
data at the most granular level

00:10:13.440 --> 00:10:15.630
as possible to us, so for
the virtual infrastructure

00:10:15.630 --> 00:10:17.040
every 20 seconds.

00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:18.510
That 20 second
granularity is great

00:10:18.510 --> 00:10:20.190
because we can do
a lot of histograms

00:10:20.190 --> 00:10:24.390
and detailed analysis on that as
well as the configuration data.

00:10:24.390 --> 00:10:26.380
We have a shared portal
view of it as well.

00:10:26.380 --> 00:10:28.320
So once it comes down,
the partners and you

00:10:28.320 --> 00:10:31.140
can work together and
collaborate on that data.

00:10:31.140 --> 00:10:32.450
And we're very non-intrusive.

00:10:32.450 --> 00:10:34.950
The only thing we need to talk
to in the virtual environment

00:10:34.950 --> 00:10:37.740
is resources like the
managers like the vCenter.

00:10:37.740 --> 00:10:39.240
We do not have to
touch the hosts.

00:10:39.240 --> 00:10:40.740
We do not have to
touch the guests.

00:10:40.740 --> 00:10:42.510
We are not using an agent.

00:10:42.510 --> 00:10:44.940
Everything we do is directly
through the VMWare APIs

00:10:44.940 --> 00:10:47.107
and they actually do the
resource collection for us,

00:10:47.107 --> 00:10:49.320
so no extra load on
the infrastructure

00:10:49.320 --> 00:10:50.770
and pulling that data in.

00:10:50.770 --> 00:10:52.820
Since we are only
looking predominately

00:10:52.820 --> 00:10:55.030
at metadata, performance
configuration,

00:10:55.030 --> 00:10:57.030
we don't have to look
into the operating system.

00:10:57.030 --> 00:10:59.040
There's no reason to go
in there unless you're

00:10:59.040 --> 00:11:02.263
doing an application discovery,
an application dependency.

00:11:02.263 --> 00:11:04.180
That would be the only
reason you'd set up it.

00:11:04.180 --> 00:11:06.347
But, if you're in a high
security or highly critical

00:11:06.347 --> 00:11:08.340
environment, we can
restrict that access

00:11:08.340 --> 00:11:10.782
and just let the
clients do its activity.

00:11:10.782 --> 00:11:12.240
Takes about five
minutes to deploy.

00:11:12.240 --> 00:11:13.985
It's quick and
easy to get going.

00:11:13.985 --> 00:11:15.360
Once it's up and
running, we have

00:11:15.360 --> 00:11:17.135
data in the portal
within 15 minutes.

00:11:17.135 --> 00:11:18.510
Like I said, we
collect that data

00:11:18.510 --> 00:11:20.890
on that 20 second granularity
and we let it keep running.

00:11:20.890 --> 00:11:22.525
So the duration of
your assessment.

00:11:22.525 --> 00:11:24.150
So if you're doing
an assessment today,

00:11:24.150 --> 00:11:26.542
tomorrow, for seven days,
we see the highs and lows

00:11:26.542 --> 00:11:27.750
over the course of that week.

00:11:27.750 --> 00:11:29.430
We know what the
weekend activity is.

00:11:29.430 --> 00:11:31.680
Nice thing is this appliance
can keep running forever.

00:11:31.680 --> 00:11:33.450
You can use the
platform and the portal

00:11:33.450 --> 00:11:34.920
as a vSphere
administrative tool.

00:11:34.920 --> 00:11:36.720
You can stage your
migrations over time,

00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:39.330
look at trends and
usage activity, peaks,

00:11:39.330 --> 00:11:43.320
99 to 95th percentiles, means
and averages of utilization

00:11:43.320 --> 00:11:46.908
of all the resources as well.

00:11:46.908 --> 00:11:49.450
Architecturally, the data that
we're collecting predominately

00:11:49.450 --> 00:11:52.360
is configuration data for
all the resources in the data

00:11:52.360 --> 00:11:56.620
center-- network, storage,
compute, your clusters

00:11:56.620 --> 00:11:59.280
and hosts and the details that
are there as well in the VMs.

00:11:59.280 --> 00:12:01.650
And we collect the
performance metadata--

00:12:01.650 --> 00:12:05.920
CPU, memory, bandwidth,
storage, I/O utilizations.

00:12:05.920 --> 00:12:08.110
We do this for both on
premise and for the cloud

00:12:08.110 --> 00:12:09.460
side of the activity.

00:12:09.460 --> 00:12:11.480
Now, it's great because,
once we have this data,

00:12:11.480 --> 00:12:13.730
we can look at configurations
and make recommendations

00:12:13.730 --> 00:12:16.833
for moving resources from
cloud to cloud very quickly.

00:12:16.833 --> 00:12:19.000
And we can also look at
right sizing and performance

00:12:19.000 --> 00:12:19.800
characteristics.

00:12:19.800 --> 00:12:22.540
Maybe this resource would be
better off on something else.

00:12:22.540 --> 00:12:24.940
Maybe we can consolidate
multiple lease resources down

00:12:24.940 --> 00:12:26.800
to a single entity,
reducing the number

00:12:26.800 --> 00:12:29.230
of instances in the cloud.

00:12:29.230 --> 00:12:30.700
All that data comes in to us.

00:12:30.700 --> 00:12:32.530
Comes in with that
20 second granularity

00:12:32.530 --> 00:12:34.420
for the virtual environment.

00:12:34.420 --> 00:12:37.590
Everything that we're bringing
in is encrypted to us.

00:12:37.590 --> 00:12:41.380
It's anonymized.

00:12:41.380 --> 00:12:43.720
Say deanonymized, but
it's anonymous to us.

00:12:43.720 --> 00:12:46.030
So all we're looking at
is just an org identifier

00:12:46.030 --> 00:12:47.890
and performance metadata.

00:12:47.890 --> 00:12:51.682
No CPU, try no IP
addresses, Mac addresses

00:12:51.682 --> 00:12:53.140
specific to most
of the assessments

00:12:53.140 --> 00:12:54.932
unless that's something
you want to enable.

00:12:57.360 --> 00:12:59.090
Now for the demo
side of it, I'm going

00:12:59.090 --> 00:13:00.220
to tell you a little bit
what we're going to do.

00:13:00.220 --> 00:13:02.190
We're going to go through
and we're going to--

00:13:02.190 --> 00:13:04.737
by default, the partners
usually have invited a customer

00:13:04.737 --> 00:13:05.820
into the platform already.

00:13:05.820 --> 00:13:08.280
So that first step, you will
actually receive an email.

00:13:08.280 --> 00:13:09.853
You'll log into the portal.

00:13:09.853 --> 00:13:12.270
And the first activity you
will do is deploy our observer.

00:13:12.270 --> 00:13:13.073
It's very quick.

00:13:13.073 --> 00:13:13.740
It's a download.

00:13:13.740 --> 00:13:15.270
There's no VA or OVF.

00:13:15.270 --> 00:13:18.710
Powers it up or set up the
connections into your cloud.

00:13:18.710 --> 00:13:21.690
If you want to add other
users, that's great.

00:13:21.690 --> 00:13:22.230
All right.

00:13:22.230 --> 00:13:24.000
The assessments in
the platform, there's

00:13:24.000 --> 00:13:25.500
hundreds and hundreds
of analytics.

00:13:25.500 --> 00:13:28.073
We categorize these
things into assessments.

00:13:28.073 --> 00:13:29.490
And assessments
in this case, this

00:13:29.490 --> 00:13:31.440
is the GCP and Intel assessment.

00:13:31.440 --> 00:13:32.910
They've gone through
and chose what

00:13:32.910 --> 00:13:34.830
characteristics that
were meaningful for them

00:13:34.830 --> 00:13:37.090
to put into this review.

00:13:37.090 --> 00:13:39.060
So as a platform,
there's a free edition

00:13:39.060 --> 00:13:40.488
and there's a premium edition.

00:13:40.488 --> 00:13:42.030
But, in the terms
of the assessments,

00:13:42.030 --> 00:13:44.197
all the analytics that are
related to the assessment

00:13:44.197 --> 00:13:46.243
are provided and funded
by Google and Intel.

00:13:46.243 --> 00:13:48.660
What we're going to have here
is in terms of the analytics

00:13:48.660 --> 00:13:50.080
are individual reports.

00:13:50.080 --> 00:13:52.650
These are what we break
out into our cards.

00:13:52.650 --> 00:13:55.163
Cards like vCenter
summary actually giving us

00:13:55.163 --> 00:13:57.330
a collaborative view of
what resources are currently

00:13:57.330 --> 00:13:59.340
in our data center.

00:13:59.340 --> 00:14:00.960
Host analysis--
what hardware's end

00:14:00.960 --> 00:14:02.682
of life, what
should be refreshed,

00:14:02.682 --> 00:14:04.140
as well as guest
operating systems.

00:14:04.140 --> 00:14:05.807
Really this really
gives us a foundation

00:14:05.807 --> 00:14:08.610
as that trusted advisor
as a partner or for Google

00:14:08.610 --> 00:14:12.060
to see the resources and make
a recommendation as we go.

00:14:12.060 --> 00:14:15.258
I'm going to start here
with that universal view.

00:14:15.258 --> 00:14:17.550
And before we actually make
the migration to the cloud,

00:14:17.550 --> 00:14:19.230
it's worth noting
what do we have.

00:14:19.230 --> 00:14:21.630
Are we looking at one cluster,
multiple data centers,

00:14:21.630 --> 00:14:25.330
multiple campuses, multiple
networks and resources.

00:14:25.330 --> 00:14:27.210
This is really more
so for everybody

00:14:27.210 --> 00:14:30.180
to get into agreement as to
what resources are out here.

00:14:30.180 --> 00:14:32.910
Really just set the tone of
did we actually go out and look

00:14:32.910 --> 00:14:34.233
at the right environment.

00:14:38.310 --> 00:14:40.440
Back us up.

00:14:40.440 --> 00:14:43.410
Host analysis offers two
functionalities for us.

00:14:43.410 --> 00:14:46.590
Give us an idea of the
resources that you have

00:14:46.590 --> 00:14:48.768
and how you've deployed
them, see the hardware.

00:14:48.768 --> 00:14:51.060
We can identify resources
that potentially could be end

00:14:51.060 --> 00:14:52.555
of life like we talked about.

00:14:52.555 --> 00:14:54.180
This opportunity to
go through and say,

00:14:54.180 --> 00:14:56.680
you know what, I've got a large
number of servers in my data

00:14:56.680 --> 00:14:59.410
center, but I'm
considering whether or not

00:14:59.410 --> 00:15:00.963
I should go to 6.7.

00:15:00.963 --> 00:15:02.880
So let's do a quick look
in the infrastructure

00:15:02.880 --> 00:15:06.780
and find out how many hosts are
not compatible with vSphere 6.7

00:15:06.780 --> 00:15:08.522
or 6.5 in this case.

00:15:08.522 --> 00:15:10.980
And I see in this organization
there are 46 hosts out there

00:15:10.980 --> 00:15:13.980
that are not compatible with
that release of the hypervisor.

00:15:13.980 --> 00:15:15.750
Now this is an
inflection point for us.

00:15:15.750 --> 00:15:17.340
Do we decide to
replace the hardware

00:15:17.340 --> 00:15:19.483
and upgrade and buy
new hypervisor licenses

00:15:19.483 --> 00:15:21.150
or is this really the
opportunity for us

00:15:21.150 --> 00:15:24.670
to move that infrastructure
to the cloud?

00:15:24.670 --> 00:15:26.830
So as this works as
a narrative for us,

00:15:26.830 --> 00:15:29.770
we'll go through
and build this up.

00:15:29.770 --> 00:15:31.830
Stepping back.

00:15:31.830 --> 00:15:32.680
Find my arrow.

00:15:32.680 --> 00:15:33.180
There we go.

00:15:38.740 --> 00:15:40.370
Guest operating
system analysis really

00:15:40.370 --> 00:15:41.940
is the on premise workload.

00:15:41.940 --> 00:15:43.760
So when we're looking
at either the cloud

00:15:43.760 --> 00:15:45.930
or we're looking at the
virtual infrastructure,

00:15:45.930 --> 00:15:47.060
these are the resources
that you currently

00:15:47.060 --> 00:15:48.518
have inside the
infrastructure that

00:15:48.518 --> 00:15:50.070
are virtualized and deployed.

00:15:50.070 --> 00:15:52.070
We see them as they're
configured, the resources

00:15:52.070 --> 00:15:53.163
they're consuming.

00:15:53.163 --> 00:15:55.580
We see the age and end of life
for some of these operating

00:15:55.580 --> 00:15:55.910
systems.

00:15:55.910 --> 00:15:57.952
So maybe there's opportunity
to move some of this

00:15:57.952 --> 00:16:01.130
to a new version of Windows,
for example, high as well

00:16:01.130 --> 00:16:03.625
as what aren't going to be
candidates for cloud migration.

00:16:03.625 --> 00:16:05.000
We're going to
see things in here

00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:08.690
like ESX, vSphere
running as a nested VM.

00:16:08.690 --> 00:16:09.920
Maybe we see the Mac OS.

00:16:09.920 --> 00:16:11.628
We'll go through and
clarify and identify

00:16:11.628 --> 00:16:13.880
resources that are not going
to be cloud candidates.

00:16:13.880 --> 00:16:16.380
Now most organizations need to
start working on the workflow

00:16:16.380 --> 00:16:17.730
and managing the content.

00:16:17.730 --> 00:16:19.370
So we break this
out to some degree

00:16:19.370 --> 00:16:21.960
based on all the metrics
that are important to you,

00:16:21.960 --> 00:16:24.290
whether it be clusters, or
hosts, or organizations,

00:16:24.290 --> 00:16:26.390
or logical containers
and resources, data

00:16:26.390 --> 00:16:29.917
stores, security groups,
state OS family and such,

00:16:29.917 --> 00:16:32.000
as well as the ability to
go through and tag these

00:16:32.000 --> 00:16:32.520
individually.

00:16:32.520 --> 00:16:34.103
So I may want to tag
a subset of these

00:16:34.103 --> 00:16:36.710
as production, staging, DevOps.

00:16:36.710 --> 00:16:39.410
We do some automatic tagging
through the infrastructure.

00:16:39.410 --> 00:16:41.990
We'll pull tags from vCenter
if they already exist.

00:16:41.990 --> 00:16:44.150
If you are doing
application collection,

00:16:44.150 --> 00:16:47.450
we'll apply the application
identity for that VM

00:16:47.450 --> 00:16:49.790
and start doing some
application based tagging,

00:16:49.790 --> 00:16:51.590
as well as guest
processes, so if you

00:16:51.590 --> 00:16:54.380
are looking at certain resources
inside of the resources

00:16:54.380 --> 00:16:56.180
as well.

00:16:56.180 --> 00:16:58.170
Now, we can use this
to tag and manage it

00:16:58.170 --> 00:17:00.617
down to a minimum subset
of resources that might

00:17:00.617 --> 00:17:01.700
want to move to the cloud.

00:17:01.700 --> 00:17:04.452
So we can tag individually
and see multiple tags.

00:17:04.452 --> 00:17:06.160
I have a couple of
them already out here.

00:17:06.160 --> 00:17:08.035
And we're going to use
that in just a second.

00:17:11.854 --> 00:17:15.690
Got to figure out which
monitor is easier to see.

00:17:15.690 --> 00:17:17.849
Ultimately, before we
make the cloud migration,

00:17:17.849 --> 00:17:21.180
there's a portion of this
that is quick costing.

00:17:21.180 --> 00:17:23.430
I want to do a quick
estimate to find out

00:17:23.430 --> 00:17:25.859
whether this is a go or no go.

00:17:25.859 --> 00:17:28.530
The effort to go through and
identify hardware and services,

00:17:28.530 --> 00:17:31.635
resources in the data
center, quantify up early

00:17:31.635 --> 00:17:33.510
and figure out is there
an opportunity for me

00:17:33.510 --> 00:17:35.493
to move these resources
takes two parts.

00:17:35.493 --> 00:17:37.410
What does it cost me on
premise and what is it

00:17:37.410 --> 00:17:39.000
going to cost me in the cloud?

00:17:39.000 --> 00:17:41.405
We do some on premise
cost simulations.

00:17:41.405 --> 00:17:43.530
There's a lot of attributes
that you can go through

00:17:43.530 --> 00:17:46.800
and tune through this as
well, but the top level ones,

00:17:46.800 --> 00:17:49.530
cost of hardware, cost of
storage, cost of memory,

00:17:49.530 --> 00:17:52.890
cost of licensing and support,
cost of third party services

00:17:52.890 --> 00:17:55.350
as well as staffing and
resources like that, our power

00:17:55.350 --> 00:17:58.110
and cooling, lots of
attributes and variables

00:17:58.110 --> 00:17:59.420
we'll go through and touch on.

00:17:59.420 --> 00:18:00.960
But what's nice
is as we quantify

00:18:00.960 --> 00:18:02.580
a total cost of the
data center, we'll

00:18:02.580 --> 00:18:03.997
actually take those
and apply that

00:18:03.997 --> 00:18:05.250
to every one of the workloads.

00:18:05.250 --> 00:18:07.710
And the end result
is for each workload,

00:18:07.710 --> 00:18:10.880
we'll quantify as a
percentage of the entire data

00:18:10.880 --> 00:18:16.180
set what is it using in terms
of storage and compute, memory.

00:18:16.180 --> 00:18:19.920
And we'll give you a cost per
workload as it is configured.

00:18:19.920 --> 00:18:22.980
Let's see if I get my
mouse on top of that.

00:18:22.980 --> 00:18:24.240
I can't see from my angle.

00:18:24.240 --> 00:18:25.110
There we go.

00:18:25.110 --> 00:18:26.970
Cost of storage,
cost to compute,

00:18:26.970 --> 00:18:27.930
giving you an overview.

00:18:27.930 --> 00:18:28.950
We do some right sizing.

00:18:28.950 --> 00:18:31.620
We can talk about some cost
avoidance and some summary

00:18:31.620 --> 00:18:33.100
details of the infrastructure.

00:18:33.100 --> 00:18:35.100
Now, what's nice is that
we have all the metrics

00:18:35.100 --> 00:18:37.560
and all the data was collected
from the infrastructure, all

00:18:37.560 --> 00:18:40.200
the IOps, all the throughputs,
all the contentions.

00:18:40.200 --> 00:18:42.250
All that data is known
for us behind the scenes.

00:18:42.250 --> 00:18:45.630
So we can actually go and do
exports and CSV and derivatives

00:18:45.630 --> 00:18:48.360
where views of other cards to
actually give you some more

00:18:48.360 --> 00:18:52.282
information on your migration.

00:18:52.282 --> 00:18:53.990
Once we've got our
costs, the second part

00:18:53.990 --> 00:18:56.850
is what's our cloud cost.

00:18:56.850 --> 00:18:58.940
Costing for the cloud's
a little bit easier.

00:18:58.940 --> 00:19:02.197
Choosing a geography, choosing
whether we want preemptive,

00:19:02.197 --> 00:19:04.280
if we want reserved
instances, we want a one year,

00:19:04.280 --> 00:19:07.610
two year commit, three
year commit storage

00:19:07.610 --> 00:19:09.493
and geography are going
to be the easy ones.

00:19:09.493 --> 00:19:10.910
But the one that
throws people off

00:19:10.910 --> 00:19:12.218
is this idea of right sizing.

00:19:12.218 --> 00:19:13.635
Obviously, every
application owner

00:19:13.635 --> 00:19:15.718
is going to ask for as
many resources as possible.

00:19:15.718 --> 00:19:18.457
If they can get 64 CPUs
and a terabyte of RAM,

00:19:18.457 --> 00:19:20.790
they're going to ask for it
whether they need it or not.

00:19:20.790 --> 00:19:22.373
But ultimately what
we're going to see

00:19:22.373 --> 00:19:23.903
is what was the
actual utilization.

00:19:23.903 --> 00:19:26.570
Where did they peak out in terms
of their actual utilization day

00:19:26.570 --> 00:19:27.380
to day?

00:19:27.380 --> 00:19:29.660
And was there an opportunity
for right sizing that?

00:19:29.660 --> 00:19:33.350
How did they perform 99% percent
of the time or 95th percentile

00:19:33.350 --> 00:19:34.880
and using those as guidelines.

00:19:34.880 --> 00:19:37.038
Because we may see a
peak out there at 100%,

00:19:37.038 --> 00:19:38.330
but it may be very short lived.

00:19:38.330 --> 00:19:40.190
It could've been a
Windows reboot, an update.

00:19:40.190 --> 00:19:41.982
Maybe it was the backup
window or something

00:19:41.982 --> 00:19:43.310
happening inside the OS.

00:19:43.310 --> 00:19:45.110
So going through and
letting you drill in

00:19:45.110 --> 00:19:50.997
and looking at actual metrics
for individual resources

00:19:50.997 --> 00:19:51.830
are available to us.

00:19:51.830 --> 00:19:54.150
So we can go look at
per hour or per second,

00:19:54.150 --> 00:19:56.680
see the type of activity that
we have had during the day.

00:19:56.680 --> 00:20:00.020
In this case, a single VM here.

00:20:00.020 --> 00:20:04.430
Can't see it's in here
how many CPUs that was.

00:20:04.430 --> 00:20:05.370
CPU and performance.

00:20:05.370 --> 00:20:05.870
Two CPUs.

00:20:05.870 --> 00:20:10.005
Used 1 and 1/2 CPUs worth
of performance at its peak.

00:20:10.005 --> 00:20:11.630
Do we right size for
that level knowing

00:20:11.630 --> 00:20:13.850
that that was a one time
event or was it something

00:20:13.850 --> 00:20:16.233
that happened over the course
of the day multiple times?

00:20:16.233 --> 00:20:17.900
And, as we start
looking over the course

00:20:17.900 --> 00:20:20.900
of a week or a month, we
can get into lots of detail.

00:20:20.900 --> 00:20:24.200
We see that that short
lived spike is very short

00:20:24.200 --> 00:20:25.017
lived universally.

00:20:25.017 --> 00:20:27.350
It's not something that we're
worrying about for our day

00:20:27.350 --> 00:20:29.740
to day production.

00:20:29.740 --> 00:20:32.410
As we've gone
through the platform,

00:20:32.410 --> 00:20:34.355
we've gone through and
