00:40:53.930 --> 00:40:56.360
I'm going to be able to go
in and see exactly what it

00:40:56.360 --> 00:41:00.110
picked for me and how it meters
to what I have on prem today.

00:41:00.110 --> 00:41:01.700
And it's full
disclosure, so you're

00:41:01.700 --> 00:41:03.957
going to see every single
costing factor that built up

00:41:03.957 --> 00:41:05.540
that pricing, including
how many hours

00:41:05.540 --> 00:41:07.623
we're buying for you if
it's an on demand catalog.

00:41:07.623 --> 00:41:10.650
And you've got the ability
to look at the committed use

00:41:10.650 --> 00:41:11.150
catalogs.

00:41:11.150 --> 00:41:15.050
It's got all the sustained
use capabilities built in.

00:41:15.050 --> 00:41:16.353
All the discounting's built in.

00:41:16.353 --> 00:41:18.020
You can get custom
discounting if you're

00:41:18.020 --> 00:41:19.312
a large customer that needs it.

00:41:19.312 --> 00:41:21.603
But, at the end of the day,
you're able to come in here

00:41:21.603 --> 00:41:23.630
and do a very realistic
assessment of what

00:41:23.630 --> 00:41:26.080
your world looks like from
comparing the private data

00:41:26.080 --> 00:41:29.900
center benchmark or your data
into any of the GCP catalogs,

00:41:29.900 --> 00:41:31.850
for example, to
see your savings.

00:41:31.850 --> 00:41:34.850
Very quick, and this is based
on actual real information

00:41:34.850 --> 00:41:37.515
from your assets
underlying the platform.

00:41:37.515 --> 00:41:39.890
And then here you'll see one
of the most important things

00:41:39.890 --> 00:41:41.598
customers really like
about the platform.

00:41:41.598 --> 00:41:44.780
It gives you the ability
to go in and look

00:41:44.780 --> 00:41:46.700
at your dependency maps.

00:41:46.700 --> 00:41:48.420
It's processed your
dependencies for you.

00:41:48.420 --> 00:41:50.670
It's going to throw these
dependencies up on a screen.

00:41:50.670 --> 00:41:51.500
It's cleaning them for you.

00:41:51.500 --> 00:41:53.300
It's taking out the
data center noise.

00:41:53.300 --> 00:41:54.800
And when I blow
this up, you'll see

00:41:54.800 --> 00:41:57.680
that it's actually telling you
how all your assets communicate

00:41:57.680 --> 00:41:59.210
with each other.

00:41:59.210 --> 00:42:01.550
This is cleansed, so it's
taken out your AD DNS

00:42:01.550 --> 00:42:03.830
noise, all the stuff that's
your usual monitoring

00:42:03.830 --> 00:42:05.000
stuff from your data center.

00:42:05.000 --> 00:42:07.430
And what it's got for you is
a very clean dependency map.

00:42:07.430 --> 00:42:09.770
This is a business server
somehow typed together,

00:42:09.770 --> 00:42:11.900
so you cannot break this
map when you migrate.

00:42:11.900 --> 00:42:16.310
This is the biggest risk area in
a customer transition to cloud.

00:42:16.310 --> 00:42:18.153
How am I going to
break my dependencies?

00:42:18.153 --> 00:42:19.570
I don't even know
my dependencies.

00:42:19.570 --> 00:42:21.350
So first, understanding
your dependencies,

00:42:21.350 --> 00:42:22.370
knowing what you're
doing with them,

00:42:22.370 --> 00:42:23.960
and seeing whether or not
you're going to break those

00:42:23.960 --> 00:42:25.010
dependencies.

00:42:25.010 --> 00:42:26.840
And, at the end of
the cycle, you're

00:42:26.840 --> 00:42:28.220
able to build out a roadmap.

00:42:28.220 --> 00:42:30.470
The roadmap is probably the
most important thing here.

00:42:30.470 --> 00:42:34.003
We will deliver a
consumable cloud roadmap.

00:42:34.003 --> 00:42:36.170
What that means it's going
